{
	title: 'Sessions in one directory hear who edits what',
	spec: 'When a session calls bash in a cwd where another open session declared or changed files (task 8w observations) in the last 15 minutes, the tool result carries a short note after the output, at most one line per other session, like "[158-abc (Fix render) is editing src/host/turns.ts, src/cmd.ts; 3 min ago]" ("just now" under a minute; at most 5 paths, then "N more"). The bash call itself counts: its declared paths are recorded before it runs and the paths that actually changed (declared and undeclared) after it ends, per session and cwd, so the neighbor hears of an edit already under way. A note is sent again only when its content (session, name, paths, not the age) changed; a neighbor that goes idle for 15 minutes, closes or leaves the cwd is forgotten, so it is announced afresh if it returns. Paths both sessions touched come first and are marked "(also yours)". Nothing is added when no other session works there. The note is part of the returned tool output (also the early result of a background call), hence of the history record, so replay and the cache see the same bytes. The index is in memory: after a host restart first notes repeat. Module: src/host/neighbors.ts, fed by fileChanges.begin/finish.',
	status: 'done',
